# General Debate on the First Supplementary Estimates for 1968–69
Tamil title: 1968-69ஆம் ஆண்டிற்கான முதலாவது துணை நிதி நிலை அறிக்கைமீது பொது விவாதம்

## Image 11

The other matter raised was industrial peace. It was said that industrialists were not acting properly and that, when they were summoned for discussions, they did not consent to attend. It is true that when an important person in an industrial concern was invited because we wanted him to assist in reaching a settlement, he hesitated to come. I was distressed by that. He later came to me and offered all his explanations. He said, “If I had come at that time, the existing situation would have become much worse; that is why I did not come.” Whatever he may say, my mind remains unsatisfied. I thank our Balasubramaniyan because his observation has helped me place that fact before this House.

Many events will bear witness that, wherever industrial peace is threatened, as soon as it is brought to my attention I summon the parties, seat both sides together, and show the greatest willingness to resolve the industrial dispute through discussion. Only last night, until one o'clock, I met those who had come to Madras before agricultural labourers could begin an agitation on the ground that they needed land. I listened properly to all their demands and concluded the talks with an undertaking to seek an appropriate remedy. Likewise, when a dispute arose last week between mirasidars and cultivators in one part of Thanjavur, I summoned both sides. Although I had been unwell for several days and was unable to speak at length, I spoke to both parties until two in the afternoon, and a form of negotiated settlement was reached. Both this Government and I have a concern for safeguarding industrial peace—

## Image 12

—and I wish to cite some instances as evidence that action is being taken for that purpose. The dispute at the Eason Group factory, the dispute at the Metal Box Company, the dispute at the Wimco factory, the dispute at Indian Forge, and the dispute at the Hindu factory: in every one of these cases my Government administration not only tried to secure a settlement, but also called upon all my friends to make an effort towards it. We are turning that into a convention, indeed a regular practice.

Two or three days ago, when handcart workers were preparing to agitate, I called my friend Dr Hande—a Swatantra Party member, a member of this House, and the MLA for that area—and sent him there with the request that he somehow settle the matter. When beedi workers and tannery workers in North Arcot district agitated, I summoned both sides, attempted a settlement, and made a way for justice to be obtained. I state this now not merely for our friend Balasubramaniyan, but also to show that there is no basis for saying that industrial peace has collapsed and no one is paying attention. I have offered several examples of the matters in which settlements have been achieved.

In some matters, conditions remain such that a settlement cannot yet be secured. Because of our patient approach, I have complete confidence that all of them can be resolved. It is in that confidence that I am approaching the Coimbatore mill problem. If only the Union Government had shown a measure of liberality concerning the newly imposed rate of excise duty—

## Image 13

—and had joined the efforts we undertook and cooperated with them, the problem could have been resolved very quickly.

But however many times we approach the Union Government, it—and particularly the Finance Minister—refuses to yield even slightly on removing the excise duty. Our State Government has made innumerable efforts for this purpose. It has enabled the mills to obtain more than two and a half crore rupees in loans by guaranteeing that the Government would repay the money should the mill owners fail to do so. Likewise, when those operating the mills said that they could not immediately pay the electricity-tax charges and needed time, instalments were granted. When they needed instalments for paying sales tax, those too were granted.

Beyond all this, only those continuously involved in the matter know the effort made to appoint a committee so that the Government could assume responsibility for the whole undertaking. How many times did we have to go to Delhi? On how many ministers' doors did we have to knock? How many times did we have to repeat the same request to those ministers? How much delay did they display in establishing those committees? To explain all this would require me to speak of certain genuine differences of view between the Union and State Governments. I therefore consider it unnecessary to give every detail. Since we assumed responsibility for the State Government, I have been determined—

## Image 14

—that no needless bitterness should arise between the Union and State Governments. Nevertheless, regarding this mill problem, I must state with regret that the Union Government has not extended cooperation commensurate with the efforts made by the State Government. We still proceed in the belief that this mill problem can be resolved if such cooperation is forthcoming in sufficient measure. That is why arrangements are now being made for three committees, instead of one, to conduct the inquiry.

My friends, whether friends from the Communist Party or the Congress Party, will all appreciate that whichever Government is formed, it can act only within the prescribed laws and regulations. If a mill does not function properly or has been closed, the Government cannot immediately declare, “We shall take it over and operate it.” Our friend Vinayakam is a lawyer. Thiru Ponnappa Nadar is a lawyer. Thiru Balasubramaniyan too is a lawyer.

**Thiru A. Balasubramaniyan:** Everyone knows that under the Constitution we do not possess the authority to take it over in that way. Nevertheless, when the livelihoods of tens of thousands of workers are being destroyed and Tamil Nadu's economy is being disrupted, we ask only that the Union Government be requested to give its assent so that the necessary law can be enacted.

**The Hon. Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** What he speaks of is justice; what I am explaining is the law. Sometimes law and justice do not correspond completely. It is my wish that they should. If the State Government possessed the right simply to take these mills over and operate them—

## Image 15

—we might have done so. Whether the Congress members would then have consented is another matter. I recall one member saying, “Since you are putting public money into this, consult us before you do it.” Thus, even if others had accepted that the Government should take over these concerns, they might not have consented. Fortunately, the public did not place me in a position where their consent must first be obtained, so I need not concern myself greatly with that question.

If our respected Congress friends cooperate in securing the removal or reduction of the excise duty, Desai may yield to some extent. A way may then open to resolve this mill problem.

There is the cinema newsreel. Ramamurthi goes to “night dances,” and yet I wondered how he had come not to like cinema. Chithan also spoke about it. The cinema industry is not merely a means of entertainment. It provides employment to tens of thousands of people. It provides opportunities for scientific equipment. It develops people's imagination and acting talent. Assessing quality and awarding prizes is therefore one of the functions a Government may perform. Just as prizes are awarded to authors of good books, as good musicians receive awards, and as good film actors are honoured, giving a prize to a good film will help raise both revenue and quality. I trust that our friend Chithan will forgive the expenditure of a little over one lakh rupees for this purpose.

**Thiru K. Vinayakam:** Can an assurance be given that no political motive will enter into it?

---NOTES---
- “மிராசுதார்கள்” is retained contextually as “mirasidars,” hereditary or recognised land-right holders in the historical Tamil agrarian system.
- Factory and company names are transliterated conservatively from the printed Tamil: Eason Group, Metal Box, Wimco, Indian Forge, and Hindu factory.
- “எக்ஸைஸ் வரி” is rendered as “excise duty.” The speech distinguishes this Union levy from State electricity tax and sales tax concessions.
- “உத்தரவாதம்” in the mill-finance passage is translated as a government loan guarantee, not a direct grant.
